 made up of a  myriad of different Christian European  traditions that would be implemented  much later the most significant and most  recognizable of these are the Christian  holidays of All Saints Day and All Souls  Day celebrated still to this day in some  European countries for example here in  Sweden we celebrate Halloween and All  Saints Day separately Halloween on  October 31st and All Saints Day on the  first Saturday of Nova  now All Saints Day is a festival of  remembrance for the Saints of  Christianity and all souls day is the  festival of remembrance for people who  have recently departed this world in  general much similar to the Mexican  festival Day of the Dead particularly in  Western Europe these twin holidays were  often celebrated around October 31st  probably as a way of appropriating the  already existing holiday of sovereign  and thus gain converts and the impact  this festival had on modern Halloween is  significant in fact historian dr.  Nicholas Rogers writes that while Cerwyn  may have been the origin of the holiday  the rituals we associate with it have  very little connection and that quote  most of these rituals developed in  conjunction with a medieval holy days of  All Souls and All Saints Day this  includes the name itself Halloween which  stems from All Hallows Eve signifying  the day before All Hallows day or All  Saints Day as well as the wearing of  costumes which appeared around the 16th  century furthermore trick-or-treating  may have origins in a practice called  soling where people went from door to  door asking for prayers for the dead  clearly then these Christian holidays  had a major impact on the modern holiday  of Halloween which shows you that its  history is very complex and multifaceted  it is true that it has its origins in  the pagan festivals Sawin but very  little of that connection remained today  for most people Halloween is a day of  partying eating candy dressing up and  watching horror films I at least very  strongly associate Halloween with some  of our most famous pop culture monsters  like Dracula Frankenstein's monster and  and werewolves and the story of these  characters is pretty interesting as well  the story of dr. Frankenstein and the  monster that he creates is one of the  most famous horror stories in the world  the movie starring Boris Karloff and the  appearance that he created is so itched  into our culture  but it's almost impossible to imagine  any other version but the original story  is actually quite different and has a  very strong message the book  Frankenstein or the modern prometheus  was written by English author Mary  Shelley in the early 19th century the  story is familiar to most of you the  scientist dr. Frankenstein wants to  create life he Rob's the graves of dead  people takes their body parts stitches  together a person and then attempts to  make it live amazingly he succeeds but  as soon as he reaches his goal he's  horrified with his own creation and  leaves him the rest of the story deals  with the monster and the pain he has to  go through being scared and isolated  without anyone to love or care for him  he's seen by everyone including his own  creator as a monster obviously this is a  very tragic and sad story and much of  this doesn't really carry over that much  to the film version more so actually in  the sequel called the Bride of  Frankenstein but nonetheless the  important part in message here is of  course that the monster isn't the  villain at all but dr. Frankenstein  himself is as well as the people who  sort of chase him and considering to be  a monster the original novel was written  in a period known as Romanticism or the  Romantic era that took place for about  the first half of the 19th century this  was an artistic intellectual and  literary period there was a strong  reaction against the period of  enlightenment that prevailed during the  preceding century the Enlightenment  period put all its emphasis on science  and rationality it rationalized  everything including nature which a lot  of people felt was inadequate  romanticism instead as a reaction to  this considers strong emotions as a  valuable aesthetic experience and  considered this to be more important  than  to rationality in a sense and so  Frankenstein or the modern prometheus is  the quintessential gothic novel of the  Romantic era because it deals with  exactly these questions the book is a  strong comment and critique of these so  excessive rationalism and scientist of  the time  dr. Frankenstein in his quest to create  life completely abandons all ethics he  cares only about whether or not he can  do it and doesn't care at all about the  creature that he actually creates he is  as Ian Malcolm says in Jurassic Park  we're so preoccupied with whether or not  they could did you stop to think they  should and this is characteristic of the  debate that this book finds itself in do  we let scientific progress compromise  our humanity are we too busy thinking  about whether or not we can that we  forget whether or not we should know  this is a theme that also carries over  to a lot of other literature for example  as I mentioned the Jurassic Park  franchise has very much the same message  throughout Mary Shelley in her book  warns against this kind of behavior dr.  Frankenstein is as we say playing God  and she wants us not to fall into that  same trap now another famous monsters of  course the werewolf of course one of the  most famous classic movie monsters is  the Wolfman  werewolves are humans that can  shape-shift into wolves and there have  been throughout history cases of  werewolf executions that is people who  are accused and tried of being  werewolves and then executed for it  much like the more famous witch trials  the origins of the werewolf myth is  difficult to know for sure it could have  been a way of dealing with serial  killers or livestock being killed by  actual wolves  some people even speculate that this had  something to do with a message against  so called interspecies sex that is that  you are not supposed to have sex with  animals in any case the werewolf has  been a very significant part of our  culture and history throughout  world and is today a very familiar  character especially in connection with  the holiday like Halloween and a lot of  those classic horror films but perhaps  my favorite creature that I associate  with Halloween is Dracula and the  vampire the vampire is an undead person  that sucks the blood of the living for  nourishment in many cases the vampire  can turn into a bat but this connection  hasn't always existed vampires have  existed particularly in European  folklore for hundreds of years if not  even longer and have taken on many  different forms as time went by the  Uniting characteristic being that they  all suck blood for nourishment and that  they are Undead in fact the classic or  should I say modern vampire was born in  the 19th century in 1819 John William  Polydorus novel the vampire was  published which is considered by many to  be the first to create a coherent genre  of vampire fiction later that century  though in 1897 the Irish author Bram  Stoker would release what is probably  the most significant novel ever for our  modern conception of vampires  he called it Dracula this classic  character vampire of vampires Dracula  was actually partly based on a real-life  person a ruler in Transylvania in the  15th century called Vlad the 3rd Dracula  more commonly known as Vlad the Impaler  it is said that Vlad killed upwards of a  hundred thousand people throughout his  reign and very characteristically he had  his enemies executed by impaling them  legend says that Vlad would sit at his  dinner table and eats as people were  brutally impaled in front of him a kind  of dinner entertainment if you will the  name Dracula actually comes from the  Romanian word that ah cool which means  the dragon Bram Stoker kept the name and  the location Transylvania but also took  a lot of influences from a European  folklore and a lot of other sort of vamp  our stories to create the most famous  vampire of all time in his novel Dracula  became a well-spoken pale man who could  turn into a bat crawl on walls couldn't  be seen in mirrors hates garlic and many  of those classic characteristics that we  know and love all these stories and  monsters are strongly associated with  Halloween and really makes it what it is  in other words Halloween is like a  melting pot of a myriad of different  influences and traditions from the pagan  festival of Sauron to the Christian  holidays of All Saints in our souls day  and finally to our modern pop culture  references like the classic horror films  and the monsters to year the episodes of  our favorite TV shows like The Simpsons  and their treehouse of horror and many  of these other influences that have come  about in the last hundred years or 200  years you could say that Halloween is  both very old and very new you can now  resume your celebrations go  trick-or-treating dress up and watch a  horror film but don't forget to stop for  a second and think about death and the  way it affects our lives it's always  going to be there but that doesn't have  to be a negative thing happy Halloween  everyone and I'll see you soon
 

